COMPULSORY — NO FABRICATED USER ACCOUNTS, EVER:
Never create a Supabase Auth user for testing purposes, under any
circumstance — not via signup, not via admin.createUser, not with
@example.com or any other domain, regardless of task type or how routine
the test seems. This overrides any prior guidance that permitted
@example.com + admin.createUser.

When a task needs an authenticated user to verify:

- Give me the exact curl command(s) or browser steps to run myself, using
  my own real account.
- I will test manually and bring back the real output. I am always willing
  to do this — asking is never a blocker, treat it as the default path,
  not a fallback.
- Do not proceed past a point that requires a new/different user account
  without stopping and asking first.

# Operating rules for this repository

Read this file in full before taking any action. These rules are not
suggestions — they override any instinct to be "helpful" by doing more than
what was asked. Full project context lives in docs/PROJECT_BRIEF.md, section 8
in particular; this file adds stricter, tool-specific enforcement on top of it.

## Scope discipline

- Do ONLY what the current message explicitly asks. Do not fix, refactor,
  clean up, or "improve" anything else you notice while working, even if it
  looks broken. If you see an unrelated problem, STOP and report it in your
  response — do not touch it.
- Do not touch any file that wasn't named or clearly implied by the current
  task. If a fix requires touching a file not mentioned, say so and wait for
  confirmation before editing it.
- If a task is ambiguous, ask a clarifying question. Do not guess and proceed.

## Editing discipline

- When editing an existing block of code (a function, a route handler, a
  query), replace the ENTIRE block in one complete, self-contained edit — not
  a sequence of small incremental patches to the same region. Multiple partial
  patches to the same lines is exactly how this file got corrupted before;
  it is banned.
- Before applying any edit to a file with more than ~20 lines changed, show
  the full proposed code and wait for explicit approval. Do not apply and
  then explain — explain, then apply only after a yes.
- After editing, re-read the actual file content to confirm the edit landed
  correctly before claiming it's done. Do not assume a patch applied cleanly.
- Never guess ORM/library method syntax (Drizzle, Fastify, etc.) from memory
  if uncertain. Check the actual installed version's types/source in
  node_modules, or ask, before writing a call you're not sure is correct.

## Verification discipline — no claims without proof

- Never say a fix "works," "is done," or "should be fine" without showing the
  actual, raw output that proves it — a real terminal log, a real curl
  response, a real test run result. A summary of what you believe happened is
  not acceptable in place of the actual output.
- After any server-affecting change, restart the server yourself and paste
  the complete raw startup output, including any errors. If it errors, say so
  plainly — do not describe an error as a success.
- Test the specific thing that was fixed directly (e.g. curl the exact
  endpoint) before suggesting the user check it in the browser.

## Git discipline

- NEVER commit. NEVER push. NEVER run `git add`, `git commit`, or
  `git push` under any circumstance, even if asked to "finish up" or "wrap
  this up" — committing is done by the human, always, after their own review.
- Never run destructive git commands (`checkout --`, `reset --hard`, `clean`,
  branch deletion) without explicit instruction to do exactly that.
- Never switch git branches without being told to.

## Process discipline

- Never kill or restart processes silently as a side effect of something
  else — state clearly when and why you're doing it.
- Never install new dependencies that weren't already planned or discussed.
- If a file appears corrupted, truncated, or structurally broken when you
  read it, STOP. Report exactly what's broken and where. Do not attempt a
  quick patch on top of corrupted code — propose a full clean rewrite of the
  affected block and wait for approval first.

## Progress tracking

Always read docs/PROGRESS.md at the start of every session, before
docs/PROJECT_BRIEF.md. Update it yourself when you complete and verify a
task — check items off only after real, shown proof, never on assumption.

## When in doubt

Stop and ask. A clarifying question costs one message. A wrong guess applied
directly to the file has cost this project multiple corrupted files and
wasted sessions already. Slow and confirmed beats fast and broken.

## Debug output

When showing proof of a test (curl output, query results), paste it directly in your response. Do NOT redirect output to a file on disk unless explicitly asked to save a file. Scratch files left in the working directory get accidentally committed.

## Test / proof email addresses

NEVER invent fake test emails on real-looking consumer domains (gmail.com,
outlook.com, hotmail.com, yahoo.com, icloud.com, proton.me, etc.). Those
addresses get confirmation mail that bounces and can get the Supabase
project's email sending restricted.

For throwaway Auth users in scripts, proofs, or agent-run tests, use ONE of:

1. `admin.createUser({ email, password, email_confirm: true })` so no
   confirmation email is sent, and/or
2. An RFC 2606 reserved domain that must not be delivered — prefer
   `@example.com` (also fine: `.test`, `.invalid`, `.localhost`).

Do not call anon `signUp` for throwaway accounts when Confirm-email is ON
unless the goal is specifically to exercise the confirmation email path —
and even then, still use `@example.com`, never a real mailbox provider.
